<h1>Getting Started</h1>
<p class="subtitle">Run an explicit scan first, then decide whether the experimental hooks and rewrites fit your workflow.</p>

<h2 id="requirements">Requirements</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Node.js {{ versions.gate.node }} or newer.</li>
  <li>Git for staged, tracked-file, history, hook, and exposure features.</li>
  <li>A disposable fixture or clean working tree when evaluating auto-fix.</li>
</ul>
<p>Direct file targets such as <code>gate scan path/to/file.js</code> can run outside a Git repository.</p>

<h2 id="install">Try or install</h2>
<div class="code-block"><span class="cmt"># Run the published package without a global install</span>
<span class="prompt">$ </span><span class="cmd">npx @penumbraforge/gate scan --all</span>

<span class="cmt"># Or install the CLI globally</span>
<span class="prompt">$ </span><span class="cmd">npm install -g @penumbraforge/gate</span>
<span class="prompt">$ </span><span class="cmd">gate version</span></div>

<div class="warning-box"><strong>A bare <code>gate</code> command is not a repository scan.</strong> On first use in a Git repository it attempts to install a pre-commit hook and prints a banner; on later use it shows status. Use <code>gate scan --all</code> for an explicit tracked-file scan.</div>

<h2 id="first-scan">Choose the scope deliberately</h2>
<div class="code-block"><span class="cmt"># All files returned by git ls-files (tracked files only)</span>
<span class="prompt">$ </span><span class="cmd">gate scan --all</span>

<span class="cmt"># Staged files; this is the default when no target flag is supplied</span>
<span class="prompt">$ </span><span class="cmd">gate scan</span>

<span class="cmt"># Explicit files or directories; directories can include untracked files</span>
<span class="prompt">$ </span><span class="cmd">gate scan src config/example.yml</span></div>

<p><code>--all</code> does not inspect untracked files because it uses <code>git ls-files</code>. Gate skips likely binary files, configured ignore patterns, and files above the configured size limit. A clean result means no enabled rule matched the inspected bytes; it is not proof that the repository contains no secrets.</p>

<h2 id="results">Read the result</h2>
<p>Gate combines bundled regular-expression patterns with entropy heuristics. It reports file, line, rule, severity, redacted context, and exposure information when available. Pattern detection can produce both false positives and false negatives.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Exit code <code>0</code>: the selected scan completed with no findings.</li>
  <li>Exit code <code>1</code>: findings remain or the scan was incomplete.</li>
  <li>Text scans currently append local audit state; JSON and SARIF scan paths do not.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="verification">Network verification is off by default</h2>
<p>Ordinary scans do not verify credentials unless <code>--verify</code> is supplied or <code>verify: true</code> is set in configuration. Verification sends the matched credential to a provider endpoint and can change severity. Read <a href="/gate/wiki/verification/">the endpoint and cache boundaries</a> before enabling it.</p>

<h2 id="setup">Optional project setup</h2>
<div class="code-block"><span class="prompt">$ </span><span class="cmd">gate init</span></div>
<p>The current initializer:</p>
<ul>
  <li>requires a <code>.git</code> entry in the current directory;</li>
  <li>detects a small set of project stack files;</li>
  <li>attempts to install both pre-commit and pre-push hooks;</li>
  <li>creates <code>.gateignore</code> only when it does not already exist; and</li>
  <li>adds <code>.env</code>, <code>.env.local</code>, <code>.env.*.local</code>, and <code>.gate/</code> to <code>.gitignore</code> when missing.</li>
</ul>
<p>It does not create <code>.gaterc</code>. It also reports hook installation as successful after calling the installer without checking each returned result, so inspect <code>.git/hooks</code> or your configured <code>core.hooksPath</code>.</p>

<h2 id="hooks">What the hooks run</h2>
<p>The pre-commit hook runs <code>gate scan --staged</code>. The pre-push hook runs <code>gate scan --changed</code>, which compares the current branch with its upstream and falls back to all tracked files when that comparison fails. Both block on a non-zero Gate exit.</p>
<p>The hook can be bypassed with <code>GATE_SKIP=1</code>; if Gate cannot be found it blocks unless <code>GATE_ALLOW_MISSING=1</code> is set. Those are deliberate escape hatches, not policy enforcement.</p>

<h2 id="remediation">Remediate with review</h2>
<div class="code-block"><span class="prompt">$ </span><span class="cmd">gate fix --dry-run</span>
<span class="prompt">$ </span><span class="cmd">gate scan --interactive</span></div>
<p>Auto-fix supports a bounded collection of source forms and can produce invalid code or mismatched environment-variable references. The vault option stores local ciphertext but does not add runtime resolution to your application. Rotate any credential that was committed or pushed; moving it out of source does not revoke it.</p>

<div class="warning-box"><strong>Protect local Gate state.</strong> Current text scans may leave plaintext credential material in the local audit record through a separate <code>secret</code> field, despite display redaction. Do not export or share <code>~/.gate/audit.jsonl</code> until you have inspected it. See <a href="/gate/wiki/security/">Security Model</a>.</div>

